Z||000000||Primaris||08-11-2001||12:10 AM||||I'm going to replace the rotors on my '88 with some cross drilled ones.  Anyone use the carbomet pads sold at Fiero Store?  I don't want a pad that will wear the rotors fast.  Because of this wish I'm thinking of going with "Green Stuff" pads which claim good performance with little rotor wear.  Any thoughts?  Also I'm concerned about the sliders.  Where, other than the fiero store, can I get them?  Should they be replaced?  New to this job since they are different on my '86<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||08-11-2001||01:45 AM||||THe carbonmet pads from fierostore work great with their rotors, I'm pretty happy with the whole thing (definately get their slider pins, yes it is worth it, in my expierence the 88s calipers tend to seize...)<P>The fierostore's stuff is probally the best STOCK rotors/pads you can buy.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||08-11-2001||02:09 PM||||On the caliper, coat the entire inside of the slider hole with silicone or synthetic brake grease. That will help prevent corrosion siezing the caliper on the slide. The slide only needs replacement when worn or damaged. <P>I think the new slides come with new seals but I'm not sure. If a soft part of the slide set is bad then it's got to be replaced. ||||reg||1
